    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ad1edff1_84b847c9",
        "filename": "tests/integration/scanner_test.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-12-07T01:19:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t understand why these are written as integration tests, rather than unit tests. Are we concerned that Binder is going to mangle our data, when that data is serialized ir deserialized?\n\nAFAICT, Binder is the only external surface used by this class. For internal surfaces, we usually test a class C by testing: i) the results returned by public methods of class C, and ii) the interaction between class C, and the other classes that C directly interacts with.\n\nSee, e.g., https://wiki.corp.google.com/twiki/bin/view/Gdh/UnitTesting\n\n(To be clear: I\u0027m not discouraging integration testing in general I just don\u0027t think it\u0027s the right tool for the job here. For example, it\u0027s more likely that we\u0027ll be able to exercise the !valid_ case in a unit test than in an integration test.)",
      "revId": "58d74c7c5ad5bf821b541f204a8610b2bfa5ab06",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
